WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.400
Remember the white-eyed fruit fly that hatched in

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:04.240
Morgan's lab in 1910?

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:07.360
He crossed it with red-eyed females, and all

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.200
their offspring had red eyes.

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:11.440
So white was recessive.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:14.400
Then he bred those offspring with each other,

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:16.720
and white eyes came back, as expected.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:18.320
But there was a twist.

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.840
Every single white-eyed fly was male.

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:24.560
Why would eye colour care whether a fly

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:26.000
is male or female?

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:29.800
The answer is in the sex chromosomes.

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:33.200
In fruit flies, as in humans, females have

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:36.240
two X chromosomes, and males have one X

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:37.600
and one Y.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:40.160
The eye colour gene is on the X,

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:41.760
and the Y doesn't carry it.

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:44.160
So a female has two copies of the

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:46.400
gene, but a male has only one.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:49.440
If that one copy is the white allele,

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:51.600
there's no other allele to hide it, so

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:53.120
his eyes are white.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:55.680
A gene on a sex chromosome is called

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:56.880
sex-linked.

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:00.060
Now Morgan's second cross.

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:02.580
The red-eyed daughters each had a red allele

00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:04.580
from their mother, and a white allele on

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:07.620
their father's X, so they were carriers.

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:09.860
Cross one with a red-eyed male.

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:12.500
He gives his X, with its red allele,

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:15.060
to every daughter, and his Y to every

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:15.540
son.

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:18.460
So every daughter has red eyes.

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:21.460
Each son's only X comes from his mother,

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:23.860
so half the sons should have white eyes.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:27.860
That matches what Morgan saw: no white-eyed daughters,

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:30.260
and close to half the sons white-eyed.

00:01:31.467 --> 00:01:33.266
People work the same way.

00:01:33.547 --> 00:01:36.986
Haemophilia A, where blood doesn't clot properly, is

00:01:37.067 --> 00:01:39.746
caused by a recessive allele on the X.

00:01:40.027 --> 00:01:42.826
A carrier mother and an unaffected father have

00:01:42.867 --> 00:01:45.466
a one in four chance, for each child,

00:01:45.547 --> 00:01:46.666
of an affected son.

00:01:47.227 --> 00:01:49.306
But for each son, the chance is one

00:01:49.387 --> 00:01:50.026
in two.

00:01:50.667 --> 00:01:54.266
So read the question: all children, or sons?

00:01:54.667 --> 00:01:56.826
No daughter of theirs is affected, because their

00:01:56.907 --> 00:01:59.066
father's X carries the normal allele.

00:01:59.547 --> 00:02:01.546
That's why conditions like this are far more

00:02:01.627 --> 00:02:04.666
common in males: a male needs one copy,

00:02:04.707 --> 00:02:06.346
but a female needs two.

00:02:07.567 --> 00:02:10.473
In a pedigree, X-linked recessive traits leave a

00:02:10.527 --> 00:02:14.286
pattern: mostly males are affected, the allele passes

00:02:14.367 --> 00:02:17.646
through unaffected carrier mothers, and it never passes

00:02:17.727 --> 00:02:18.846
from father to son.

00:02:19.327 --> 00:02:21.606
But it doesn't only come from mothers.

00:02:21.807 --> 00:02:24.366
An affected father passes his X to every

00:02:24.567 --> 00:02:27.126
daughter, so all his daughters are carriers.

00:02:27.327 --> 00:02:29.566
And one quick test rules it out: an

00:02:29.807 --> 00:02:32.246
affected daughter with an unaffected father.

00:02:32.447 --> 00:02:34.606
That's the family from the last video, so

00:02:34.687 --> 00:02:37.086
that condition wasn't X-linked recessive.

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:41.180
So far, one allele has always been fully

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:44.940
dominant, and two heterozygotes gave 3 to 1.

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:47.420
But that's not the only possibility.

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:49.580
The fastest way to tell the patterns apart

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:52.180
is to read the heterozygote first.

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:54.700
Does it look like one of the homozygotes,

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:56.940
does it look in between, or does it

00:02:57.020 --> 00:02:58.380
show both at once?

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:02.080
Snapdragons show the in-between kind.

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:04.480
Cross a red one with a white one,

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:06.440
and every offspring is pink.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:08.800
The red allele codes for an enzyme that

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:09.920
makes red pigment.

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:12.920
The white allele's enzyme doesn't work, like the

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:15.280
white snapdragons in the proteins video.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:18.160
One working copy makes less pigment, so the

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:19.040
flowers are pink.

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:21.480
That's incomplete dominance.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:23.920
Cross two pinks, and you get 1 red

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.880
to 2 pink to 1 white.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:28.400
Red and white come back, so the alleles

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:29.520
never blended.

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:30.720
Only the colour did.

00:03:31.934 --> 00:03:33.373
Cattle show the other kind.

00:03:33.454 --> 00:03:36.093
Cross a red bull with a white cow,

00:03:36.134 --> 00:03:37.853
and the calves are roan.

00:03:38.174 --> 00:03:39.933
Look closely at a roan coat.

00:03:40.094 --> 00:03:40.973
It isn't pink.

00:03:41.534 --> 00:03:43.933
Each hair is either fully red or fully

00:03:44.014 --> 00:03:45.293
white, mixed together.

00:03:45.854 --> 00:03:48.973
Both alleles are fully expressed, side by side.

00:03:49.454 --> 00:03:51.053
That's co-dominance.

00:03:51.374 --> 00:03:53.693
Two roans give 1 red to 2 roan

00:03:53.854 --> 00:03:56.093
to 1 white, the same ratio as the

00:03:56.174 --> 00:03:59.933
snapdragons, because in both cases the heterozygote looks

00:04:00.134 --> 00:04:02.013
different from both homozygotes.

00:04:03.234 --> 00:04:06.353
Your ABO blood group adds one more twist.

00:04:06.834 --> 00:04:09.953
In the population, this gene has three alleles,

00:04:10.034 --> 00:04:10.593
not two.

00:04:11.074 --> 00:04:12.593
That's multiple alleles.

00:04:13.074 --> 00:04:15.313
But each person still carries only two of

00:04:15.394 --> 00:04:15.633
them.

00:04:16.194 --> 00:04:19.473
One allele's enzyme puts A antigens, small sugar

00:04:19.554 --> 00:04:21.873
markers, on your red blood cells.

00:04:22.114 --> 00:04:24.273
Another puts B antigens on.

00:04:24.754 --> 00:04:27.713
The third, small i, makes an enzyme that

00:04:27.794 --> 00:04:30.033
doesn't work, so it adds neither.

00:04:30.674 --> 00:04:33.793
A and B are co-dominant: with both, you're

00:04:33.874 --> 00:04:34.913
type AB.

00:04:35.554 --> 00:04:38.433
Both are dominant over small i, so type

00:04:38.594 --> 00:04:41.073
O means two small i alleles.

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:43.740
Here's the surprise.

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:46.140
A type A parent and a type B

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:48.380
parent can have a child of any blood

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:49.260
group.

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:51.860
If each carries a hidden small i, the

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:55.660
square has four boxes: AB, A, B and

00:04:55.820 --> 00:04:58.540
O, each with a one in four chance.

00:04:59.767 --> 00:05:04.486
To remember the three relationships: incomplete is in-between,

00:05:04.567 --> 00:05:06.646
and co shows both.

00:05:06.727 --> 00:05:09.366
And for sex linkage: Dad gives his X

00:05:09.407 --> 00:05:12.086
to daughters, and his Y to sons.

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:14.900
Two harder questions now.

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:18.820
First, red-green colour blindness is caused by a

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:21.220
recessive allele on the X chromosome.

00:05:21.940 --> 00:05:24.260
A woman with normal vision, whose father was

00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:27.060
colour-blind, has children with a man with normal

00:05:27.140 --> 00:05:27.620
vision.

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:30.580
What's the probability that their first child is

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:31.780
a colour-blind son?

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:34.500
What's the probability that a son of theirs

00:05:34.580 --> 00:05:35.860
is colour-blind?

00:05:35.980 --> 00:05:38.820
And could a daughter of theirs be colour-blind?

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:39.780
Explain.

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:41.460
Pause here to answer.

00:05:51.867 --> 00:05:54.426
Her father gave her his only X, with

00:05:54.467 --> 00:05:56.906
the colour-blind allele, so she's a carrier.

00:05:57.547 --> 00:06:00.106
Each son gets his X from her, so

00:06:00.187 --> 00:06:02.266
a son has a one in two chance.

00:06:02.827 --> 00:06:05.066
Among all their children, that's a one in

00:06:05.187 --> 00:06:07.146
four chance of a colour-blind son.

00:06:07.867 --> 00:06:10.666
Every daughter gets her father's normal X, so

00:06:10.747 --> 00:06:12.346
no daughter can be colour-blind.

00:06:12.907 --> 00:06:14.346
If you said one in two for the

00:06:14.427 --> 00:06:17.386
first child, you answered for sons, not all

00:06:17.467 --> 00:06:18.106
children.

00:06:19.334 --> 00:06:19.893
Second.

00:06:20.374 --> 00:06:22.373
A mother with type A blood and a

00:06:22.454 --> 00:06:24.933
father with type B blood have a child

00:06:25.014 --> 00:06:26.293
with type O blood.

00:06:26.934 --> 00:06:28.933
Work out the genotypes of all three.

00:06:29.574 --> 00:06:32.293
Then find the probability that their next child

00:06:32.374 --> 00:06:34.053
has type AB blood.

00:06:34.614 --> 00:06:35.973
Pause here to answer.

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:50.000
Type O means two small i alleles, so

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:52.280
the child got one from each parent.

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:54.320
So the mother is type A with a

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:56.880
hidden small i, and the father is type

00:06:57.040 --> 00:06:58.720
B with a hidden small i.

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:03.120
Their square gives AB, A, B and O,

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:05.360
so the chance of type AB is one

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:06.080
in four.

00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:08.840
If you said a type A and a

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:11.120
type B parent can't have a type O

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:14.080
child, remember that each can carry a hidden

00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:15.120
small i.

00:07:16.334 --> 00:07:18.173
So when a cross doesn't give 3 to

00:07:18.254 --> 00:07:21.093
1, read the heterozygote first.

00:07:21.374 --> 00:07:24.013
In between means incomplete dominance.

00:07:24.254 --> 00:07:26.333
Both showing means co-dominance.

00:07:26.734 --> 00:07:28.973
More than two alleles in the population means

00:07:29.134 --> 00:07:30.373
multiple alleles.

00:07:30.574 --> 00:07:33.293
And if sons and daughters show different ratios,

00:07:33.374 --> 00:07:35.293
look for a gene on the X.

00:07:35.614 --> 00:07:38.173
Next, you'll use all of these on unfamiliar

00:07:38.254 --> 00:07:39.613
crosses and pedigrees.

00:07:40.834 --> 00:07:42.273
Here are the key notes.

00:07:42.514 --> 00:07:44.433
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:44.514 --> 00:07:45.313
to copy them.
